# Changelog

## 1.3.6
**The line-input normalling is removed for good and the input stage is back to a pure, symmetric
stereo path.** The passive mono normal that 1.3.4 added (`R52` + the `J19` link) is deleted, and the
two input bias returns are re-derived so that the left and right channels are the same circuit: with
no jumper fitted or bridged (as shipped) each channel is completely independent, there is **zero**
cross-coupling between the left and right signal paths, the two channels have matched loading and
matched level, and both input nodes sit on the 1.65 V `+2.5V` node. (The mono mode that replaces the
old normal is a **selector jumper at the right line amplifier's input**, added by the second commit
of this revision.)

* **`R52` (2 M) and its link `J19` are removed** from the schematic and from the PCB, together with
  the net `NORM_R`, `J19`'s `IN_L` stub and the whole `BUFF_IN_R` branch that only fed `R52` (F.Cu
  stub `(233.690, 91.900)-(233.700, 88.500)`, B.Cu trunk `(233.700, 88.500)-(247.600, 88.500)`,
  B.Cu jog `(247.600, 88.500)-(247.600, 88.265)` and the via at `(233.700, 88.500)`). `BUFF_IN_R`
  keeps the existing `U2.6`/`U2.7 -> RV1.3` path, so nothing is left dangling.
* **`R5` 2 M becomes the right channel's DC bias return: its pin 1 moves from `BUFF_IN_L` to the
  1.65 V node `+2.5V`** (pin 2 stays on `IN_R`); `R24` 2 M keeps its 1.3.4 role and remains the left
  channel's bias return (`IN_L` -> `+2.5V`). Both channels are therefore identical: the same 2 M
  return to `+2.5V` at the input node, the same 1 M series resistor into the line amplifier (`R17` /
  `R16`) and the same 680 k feedback (`R19` / `R18`). Numbers: input impedance
  `2 M || 1 M = 667 kOhm` on both channels (**0.000 dB** difference), gain `-680k/1M = -0.68 =
  -3.35 dB` on both (**0.000 dB** difference), and since both nodes sit on the same 1.65 V bias
  there is no DC current in `R17`/`R16`, so the amplifier outputs sit at 1.65 V and the 1.65 V bias
  level and `+2.5V` net name are unchanged. The MCP6002 input bias current (<= 20 pA x 2 M = 40 uV
  worst case) is negligible; the resistors are the already-sourced C11512 part.
* **Why 2 M and not the v1.3.2 10 k:** a 2 M return does not load the 500 Ohm bias source (the node
  sits at the true 1.65 V and no offset is injected through `R17`/`R16`), it does not load the source
  (input impedance 667 kOhm instead of 9.9 kOhm), it keeps `R24`/`R5` at their existing value and
  part number, and both channels stay exactly matched.
* **PCB.** `R5` is not moved; only its pin-1 copper changes. `R5.1` is joined to the `+2.5V` B.Cu
  rail by one 0.2 mm F.Cu stub, one 0.7/0.3 mm via and two 0.2 mm B.Cu segments
  (`238.125,85.725 -> 238.25,85.60`; via at `238.25,85.60`; `-> 238.00,86.00 -> 238.00,90.062`, the
  last one landing on the existing `+2.5V` horizontal at `y = 90.062`). The B.Cu link crosses the
  `IN_L` trace on the other layer: an all-F.Cu connection is impossible because the `IN_L` trace
  walls that pocket off (the same conclusion 1.3.4 recorded for the buffered re-tap).
* **Latent mis-coded return stub corrected.** The existing `(241.929, 64.643)-(240.411, 64.643)`
  F.Cu segment (R24's `+2.5V` return, which has been on `+2.5V` at both ends since 1.3.4) was still
  stored with the old net code `1` (`GND`). Editing the PCB net table exposes it, so its one code
  digit is corrected to `18` (`+2.5V`). No geometry change.
* **Netlist delta (exactly one net disappears).** `NORM_R` = {`R52.1`, `J19.2`} is gone: the
  schematic netlist goes 81 -> 80 entries. `+2.5V` gains `R5.1`; `BUFF_IN_L` loses `R5.1` and is
  {`RV2.3`, `U2.1`, `U2.2`}; `IN_L` loses `J19.1` and is {`INPUT1.1`, `R17.2`, `R24.1`, `U2.3`};
  `IN_R` is unchanged at {`INPUT1.2`, `R16.2`, `R5.2`, `U2.5`}. Every other net, pin and value is
  unchanged. Verbatim check: the two channels now share only `GND`, `+2.5V` and `+3.3V`; a netlist
  graph search over the passive parts (resistors, capacitors, pots, jumpers; ICs excluded because
  their internal path is intentional and per-channel) finds **no path from the left chain to the
  right chain**, while the same search on 1.3.5 finds `BUFF_IN_L -R5- IN_R` and
  `BUFF_IN_R -R52- NORM_R -J19- IN_L`.
* **Verification.** `kicad-cli sch erc` == baseline (11 findings, identical kinds/severities, no new
  ones). `kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity` == **0 errors, 0 unconnected, 145 parity items
  (== baseline)**, 80 violations = 71 `lib_footprint_mismatch` (baseline 73 minus the two removed
  footprints) + the 5 `silk_overlap` and 4 `silk_over_copper` items already present in the baseline
  (no new silkscreen item, no part was moved). The B.Cu GND zone was re-poured: only one of its 49
  filled polygons changed (clearance channel for the new B.Cu link plus the copper regained from the
  deleted `R52`/`J19` tracks), which accounts for 1503 of the 2057 changed lines in the board file.
* **Out of scope and proven unchanged** (block-by-block text comparison against 1.3.5): `U3`,
  `R6`/`R7`, `RV1`/`RV2`, `C3`/`C7`/`C8`/`C9`/`C10`/`C11`/`C12`/`C13`, `R8`/`R9`/`R10`/`R12`,
  `J6` (headphone jack), `J1` (line jack), `C18`/`C19`, `R20`/`R21`, the loud-gain parts
  `R53`/`R54` + `J20`/`J21`, `R16`/`R17`/`R18`/`R19`, `U2`/`U4`, `R14`/`R15`, the 1.65 V bias
  network, the jack footprints and every `gr_*`/Edge.Cuts item are byte-identical. `R5`'s footprint
  block differs only in its pad-1 net line; `R24`'s is identical.
* **Headphone consequence.** The headphone stage taps a different node - `BUFF_IN_L`/`BUFF_IN_R`
  through `RV2`/`RV1` and `R7`/`R6` into `U3A`/`U3B`, not the line-out node - so this revision
  changes nothing there and the headphone output stays stereo in both selector positions.
* **Still true (unchanged, tracked separately):** the stale production exports / pinned fingerprint
  issue documented in `production/EXPORT_BLOCKER.md`; `tools/regenerate_production.py` still refuses
  to re-export because `base.kicad_pcb` does not match the `routed_pcb_sha256` pin in
  `verification/fixed-geometry.json`. When that pin is re-baselined, `R5`/`R24` collapse into the
  existing C11512 BOM line and `R52`/`J19` disappear from the excluded set.

## 1.3.5
**Line-out loud-gain option (Phase 2 of the line-out/level work, the item 1.3.4 recorded as
deferred): each channel now carries a series resistor with a shunt solder jumper across it, so
the line-out feedback can be switched from 680 k to 1.68 M on the bench.** New parts and copper
plus the matching schematic edit; no existing value, footprint position or routing changes.

* **The two states, per channel.** `J20` (left) / `J21` (right) sit **in the inverting-node track**
  and are **bridged as shipped** (`Jumper:SolderJumper-2_P1.3mm_Bridged_Pad1.0x1.5mm`, the exact
  footprint of `J19`). Bridged = the shunt is fitted = the node is continuous = feedback stays
  `R19`/`R18` = **680 k = +6.28 dB vs the 330 k board (unchanged from 1.3.3/1.3.4, this is what
  ships)**. Cutting the 0.3 mm copper bridge = the shunt is removed = the node is broken and the
  only path left is through the new series resistor: feedback = 680 k + 1 M = **1.68 M =
  +14.14 dB vs the 330 k board, i.e. +7.86 dB vs today's default**. Both states are
  **closed-loop**: the summing node always keeps a DC path to the op-amp output (through
  `R19`/`R18` alone when bridged, through `R19`+`R53` / `R18`+`R54` when cut), and `R17`/`R16`
  keep the input path.
* **New `R53`/`R54` = 1 M (0402).** `0402WGF1004TCE` / LCSC `C26083` - the part already used by
  `R16`/`R17`, so **no new BOM line**. They are spliced into the inverting-node trunk
  (`238.44,56.388`-`238.44,61.153` on the left, `248.982,56.261`-`248.982,61.153` on the right),
  which is cut between `y=58.4` and `y=59.7`; the two branch tracks (F.Cu, 0.3 mm) run from
  `R53`/`R54` pad 1/2 into `J20`/`J21` pad 1/2. Both new parts are SMD 0402 / SMD jumper with
  `F.Cu` copper only - no via, no hole, no other layer is touched.
* **The series resistor is not the 820 k 0402 that 1.3.4 looked for.** No 0402 820 k part could
  be verified, and the spec-exact 0603 (C23252) does not fit the pocket, so the board keeps the
  already-sourced 1 M `C26083` and lands at **1.68 M / +14.14 dB** (the design target was
  "~+15 dB"); the divider is 680 k / 1 M in both channels, so the two states are 6.28 dB and
  14.14 dB above the original 330 k board.
* **Schematic.** `R53`/`R54` (`Device:R`, 1 M, C26083, `Datasheet "~"`) are inserted in series in
  the `R17.pin1`->`U4.2` / `R16.pin1`->`U4.6` wire; `J20`/`J21`
  (`Connector_Generic:Conn_01x02`, Value `SolderJumper_2_Bridged`, `in_bom no`, `Datasheet ""`)
  bridge them from below. Four junctions were added and four **global** labels name the split
  nodes: `LFB_A`/`LFB_B` and `RFB_A`/`RFB_B` (line-out feedback node, side A = the
  `R19`/`R18` + op-amp-input side, side B = the `R17`/`R16` side).
* **Netlist delta (exactly two nets per channel, nothing else).** `Net-(U4A--)` is renamed
  **`LFB_A`** = {`R19.2`, `U4.2`} + the new `R53.1`, `J20.1`, and the new net **`LFB_B`** =
  {`R17.1`} + `R53.2`, `J20.2`. Likewise `Net-(U4B--)` -> **`RFB_A`** = {`R18.2`, `U4.6`,
  `R54.1`, `J21.1`} and **`RFB_B`** = {`R16.1`, `R54.2`, `J21.2`}. The schematic netlist goes
  from 79 entries (78 signal nets + `GND`) to 81 (80 signal nets + `GND`); **every other net,
  pin and value is unchanged** (verified by exporting both netlists and diffing the pin sets).
  In the PCB the net table goes 80 -> 82 entries: net 33/30 keep their codes and are renamed,
  net 80 = `LFB_B` and net 81 = `RFB_B` are new.
* **Numbers.** Default (bridged): 680 k/1 M = 0.68 = -3.35 dB, +6.28 dB vs the 330 k board.
  Loud (cut): 1.68 M/1 M = 1.68 = +4.51 dB, +14.14 dB vs the 330 k board and +7.86 dB vs the
  shipped default. The 1.68 M feedback does **not** change the output impedance - that is set by
  `R20`/`R21` (1 k) and the op-amp's closed-loop output impedance - it raises the noise gain from
  1.68 to 2.68 (+4.06 dB, so the op-amp's own noise and the bias-rail noise rise with the
  signal) and lowers the small-signal bandwidth from ~595 kHz to ~373 kHz (GBW 1 MHz / noise
  gain), still far above the audio band. DC: the MCP6002's CMOS input current (~1 pA x 1.68 M =
  ~1.7 uV) is negligible, and `C19`/`C18` block the output offset anyway.
* **Verification.** `kicad-cli sch erc` == baseline (11 findings, same kinds/severities, no new
  ones). `kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity` == **0 errors, 0 unconnected, 145 parity items
  (== baseline)**, 82 violations = 73 `lib_footprint_mismatch` (baseline 69 + the 4 new
  footprints, the pre-existing library-copy class) + **9 silkscreen warnings, all listed here
  because they are the only new items**: `J20`'s silk box and `J21`'s silk box clip `R17`'s and
  `R16`'s existing reference text (5 `silk_overlap` items), and `R16`'s / `R17`'s / `C20`'s
  existing reference text sits over `J21` pad 2, `R54` pad 2, `J20` pad 2 and `R53` pad 2
  (4 `silk_over_copper` items). All of them are reference-text placement only; no copper,
  clearance, mask or connectivity issue, and no existing part was moved to avoid them.
* **Out of scope and proven unchanged.** `U3`, `R6`/`R7`, `RV1`/`RV2`, `C18`/`C19`, `R20`/`R21`,
  `HEAD_OUT_L`/`HEAD_OUT_R`, `+2.5V` (name and network), the 1.65 V bias parts and the jack
  footprints are byte-identical (block-by-block text comparison against 1.3.4); `R18`/`R19` keep
  the 680 k value and their 0603 footprints; every `gr_*`/Edge.Cuts item, the outline, mounting
  holes and jack positions are byte-identical; the Phase-1 parts (`R5`, `R24`, `R52`, `J19`, and
  the removal of `R25`) are untouched - their footprint blocks differ only in the net-name lines
  that this revision renamed.
* **Pin-header variant (FYI, not implemented):** no. The only free pocket beside `R19`/`R17` and
  `R18`/`R16` is 3.79 mm tall between the two 0603 courtyards, while a 2-pin 2.00 mm header in the
  `J7..J18` style needs ~5.0 mm of courtyard plus 1.35 mm through-hole pads that would clash with
  `R19`/`R18` and `U4`'s pads at the only in-line position; the same conclusion 1.3.4 recorded.
* **Still true (unchanged, tracked separately):** the stale production exports / pinned
  fingerprint issue - `tools/regenerate_production.py` still refuses to re-export because
  `base.kicad_pcb` does not match the `routed_pcb_sha256` pin in
  `verification/fixed-geometry.json`. When that pin is re-baselined, `R53`/`R54` collapse into the
  existing C26083 BOM line and `J20`/`J21` (bare copper pads, no part ordered) join the excluded
  set with `J19`.

## 1.3.4
**Line-input normalling: the leaky `R25` bridge is gone, the mono normal is unilateral, and a
normalling-disable link is fitted.** PCB work (new parts and copper), the first phase of the
line-out/loud-jumper work that 1.3.3 listed as pending.

* **`R25` (10 k, 0402) is removed.** It bridged `IN_L` and `IN_R` with a plain resistor, which is
  bidirectional: measured as ~21 dB of channel separation and (with `R24`) a ~0.83 dB L/R level
  imbalance. Nothing else is left dangling - `IN_L` keeps `INPUT1.1`, `R24.1`, `R17.2`, `U2.3` and
  `IN_R` keeps `INPUT1.2`, `R16.2`, `R5.2`, `U2.5`.
* **New `R52` = 2 M (0402, C11512) is the mono normal, and it is unilateral**: `BUFF_IN_R` (the
  U2B follower output, a low-impedance driver) -> `R52` -> `J19.2` -> `IN_L`. The right channel can
  therefore never be modulated by the left one, and when both inputs are patched the leak is set only
  by the destination node's source impedance: 1 k/(1 k + 2 M) = **-66 dB** (was -21 dB).
* **`R24` 10 k -> 2 M and its return moved from `GND` to the 1.65 V bias node `+2.5V`.** This is the
  DC return the clamped normal needs: `IN_L` and `BUFF_IN_R` now sit at the same 1.65 V, so the
  normal link carries no DC current and no offset is injected. It also removes the old imbalance -
  `R24` no longer loads only the left source (0.002 dB of L/R difference, was 0.83 dB).
* **`R5` 1 M -> 2 M.** `R5` is the pre-existing left->right unilateral normal (`BUFF_IN_L` ->
  `IN_R`, the same convention `modules/head_out` uses for its input normalling); at 2 M its leak goes
  from -60.0 dB to -66.0 dB, so the worst of the two links is 66 dB - 6 dB inside the 60 dB target.
* **`J19` is the normalling-disable link: a 2-pad solder jumper** (`Jumper:SolderJumper-2_P1.3mm_Bridged_Pad1.0x1.5mm`),
  i.e. the same "bridged = normal enabled as shipped / cut the bridge = L and R independent" polarity
  the design calls for. The first choice was a 2.00 mm pin header in the `J7..J18` style, but there
  is **no room**: a clearance scan of the whole audio block finds no position where a 1.35 mm
  through-hole pad clears the surrounding nets on both layers (the 2.00 mm headers live in the slot
  bands, not in the audio block), so the solder-jumper fallback is used explicitly. It is
  copper-only: the footprint carries `exclude_from_bom`/`exclude_from_pos_files` and the symbol is
  `in_bom no`.
* **Netlist delta (exactly one new net):** `NORM_R` = {`R52.1`, `J19.2`}; `IN_L` -`R25.1` +`J19.1`;
  `IN_R` -`R25.2`; `+2.5V` +`R24.2`; `GND` -`R24.2`. Every other net, pin and value is unchanged.
* **Headphone block untouched:** `U3`, `R6`/`R7`, `RV1`/`RV2`, `HEAD_OUT_L`/`HEAD_OUT_R` and
  everything downstream are byte-identical (the per-file diff contains no line mentioning them).
* **Verification:** `kicad-cli sch erc` == baseline (11 findings, same kinds, no new ones);
  `kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity` == **0 errors, 0 unconnected, 145 parity items** (the
  baseline has 146; the one that disappeared was `R25`'s `Datasheet '~' vs ''`). Remaining warnings
  are the pre-existing `lib_footprint_mismatch` class (69 vs 68: the two new footprints are copies of
  the board's own customised library copies, like the 68) and 2 new `silk_over_copper` items (R52's
  reference field and R15's reference field over R52's pad mask openings). The F.Cu GND zones were
  re-poured (KiCad 10 filler, polygons written back in the file's own 20241229 format), which is the
  only other change in `base.kicad_pcb`.
* **Numbers:** channel separation >= 66 dB in normal mode (target >= 60 dB); mono-normal level
  -12.0 dB for `R` patched only and -6.0 dB for `L` patched only. The level is set by the 1 M
  `R17`/`R16` input loading; a unity-level normal needs the `R16`/`R17` re-tap to the buffered nodes,
  which was evaluated and rejected for copper reasons (it needs a ~24 mm run through the `IN_L`/
  `IN_R` corridor with no viable bottom-layer route: the +3.3 V and +2.5 V bottom runs block every
  vertical corridor, and the diagonal pair runs 0.7 mm apart). Recorded here so the trade-off is
  explicit: 66 dB of separation costs ~10 dB of normal level.
* **Still true (unchanged, tracked separately):** the stale production exports / pinned fingerprint
  issue. `tools/regenerate_production.py` refuses to re-export because `base.kicad_pcb` no longer
  matches the `routed_pcb_sha256` pin in `verification/fixed-geometry.json`. When that pin is
  re-baselined, `R5`/`R24`/`R52` collapse into one BOM line (C11512) and `J19` (bare copper pads, no
  part ordered) joins the excluded set.

* **Phase 2 (the line-out loud-gain shunt) is NOT in this revision - recorded as deferred, not
  half-applied.** The designed change is: keep `R18`/`R19` = 680 k and add one series resistor per
  channel so the feedback totals ~1.5 M, with a shunt across the added part (shunt fitted = 680 k =
  +6.28 dB, exactly as shipped here; shunt removed = 1.5 M = +13.15 dB) - the amplifier stays
  closed-loop in both states. Two concrete blockers are recorded: (1) **no verified part.** The only
  820 k part that could be confirmed is the 0603 `0603WAF8203T5E` / C23252, and a 0603 does not fit
  the only free slot beside `R18`/`R19` (its ~3.1 x 1.86 mm courtyard overlaps `R19`'s or `R18`'s
  courtyard in the in-line vertical position). The board's already-sourced 0402 alternative is the
  1 M `C26083` (680 k + 1 M = 1.68 M = +14.1 dB, i.e. ~1 dB above the "~13.1 dB" target); a 0402
  820 k LCSC code could not be verified. (2) **layout.** It needs an in-line cut of both inverting-node
  tracks (`238.44/56.39`-`238.44/61.15` and `248.98/56.26`-`248.98/61.15`) plus a ~3.3 x 2.5 mm shunt
  placeholder in the only two free pockets (approx. `238.5..241.5 x 57.5..60.5` and
  `249.5..252.5 x 57.2..60.9`), i.e. a second layout + ERC/DRC cycle of the same size as this one.
  Nothing of Phase 2 exists in this revision: no footprint, copper or net was added for it, so the
  board is left in one clean, fully verified state.
## 1.3.3
**Line-out output level, step 1 of the agreed fixes: the default gain is raised.**
`R18`/`R19` 330 k -> 680 k (0603, same footprints/placement/geometry). The line-out
stage is the inverting pair around `U4` with 1 M sense resistors (`R16`/`R17`), so the
voltage gain goes from |330k/1M| = 0.33 to |680k/1M| = 0.68, i.e. **+6.28 dB relative to the previous 330 k board** (20*log10(0.68/0.33)) at the
line-out jacks. No other value, net, reference, footprint or copper feature changes.

* **Implemented here:** the 680 k default level fix, in both `base.kicad_sch` and
  `base.kicad_pcb` (value properties only; copper untouched, R20/R21 stay 1 k).
* **Still pending (designed, not yet in the files):** the gain-select shunt jumper that
  selects the loud state (680 k + 820 k in series = 1.50 M -> +13.15 dB, i.e. +13.15 dB relative to the previous 330 k board), the
  removal of the `R25` 10 k `IN_L`-`IN_R` bridge, the `R16`/`R17` re-tap from
  `BUFF_IN_L`/`BUFF_IN_R`, and the normalling-disable jumper. These need new parts and
  copper and are *not* in this revision; the shipped board is single-state at +6.28 dB.
* **Headphone block untouched:** this revision changes only the two `Value`
  properties of `R18`/`R19`; `U3`, `R6`/`R7`, `RV1`/`RV2` and `HEAD_OUT_*` are
  byte-identical (the diff contains no occurrence of them).
* **Verification for this change:** `kicad-cli sch erc` == baseline (11 findings, same
  kinds); `kicad-cli pcb drc --schematic-parity` == baseline violations with 0
  unconnected items (the KiCad 10.0.6 baseline carries 68 `lib_footprint_mismatch`
  warnings and 146 `Datasheet '~' vs ''` parity warnings that are also present on the
  unmodified board; KiCad 9.0.8 recorded 0 parity items).

## 1.3.2
Fab-ready release: the hand-solder lines are recorded explicitly and the mated
stack height is calculated from datasheet dimensions. **No part, value, footprint, reference
designator, net or copper feature changed** (`base.kicad_pcb` stays
byte-identical, `9fc20400…`); the schematic is untouched too.

* **Scope correction from the owner:** the module sockets are **hand-soldered by
  the builder**, not ordered from or assembled by JLCPCB. Connector gender is an
  assembly note, not an ordering blocker, so the DO-NOT-ORDER hold is lifted.
* **JLCPCB assembly lines are unchanged at 119 SMD placements / 32 BOM rows**:
  the exporter has always rejected every through-hole footprint, so
  `VSUPPLY_1..12`, `GND1..12`, `INPUT1`, `5V14`, `J7..J18`, the four 3.5 mm jacks
  and the two RV09 pots were never on them. `C2894928` appears on **no**
  assembly line.
* The 44 non-SMD references (25 1x05 sockets + the 2x05 `5V14` + 12 rail-select
  headers + 4 jacks + 2 pots) are now a first-class, checked list:
  `production/hand-solder.json` (hand-maintained input, SHA-256 recorded in the
  manifest), `production/hand-solder.csv` (generated one-row-per-reference list:
  `Qty` = 1 per row, `GroupQty` = that group's size, 44 rows total) and guards in
  `tools/regenerate_production.py` that fail the export on a duplicate reference,
  a group/qty mismatch, an incomplete or over-long list, any hand-solder ref on
  the JLCPCB BOM/CPL, or an exporter exclusion set that differs from the list.
* **The legacy male part number is off the generated BOM without touching
  KiCad.** `LCSC`/`MPN` on the socket symbols cannot be re-substituted without
  also rewriting the same fields inside the pinned, byte-identical
  `base.kicad_pcb`, so the documented per-reference override rewrites only the
  generated `production/bom.csv` to `NOT-JLC`, the convention the jacks and pots
  already use. `C2894928` and `C2894966` no longer appear anywhere in the BOM.
* **Base sockets must be female 1x05 2.54 mm**: 28 module PCBs exist, 27 were
  inspected, and **25 of them carry their power/ground connection as a male
  `Conn_01x05_Pin` header on the bottom side** (4mix and imix mount the same
  header on `F.Cu`; `32bit` cannot be loaded standalone). Recommended builder part: **C2897368 / PM254-1-05-Z-8.5** (HCTL,
  8.5 mm insulator, 6 155 in stock, $0.0826 @150+). Shunt: **C5664** (BOOMELE
  2.00 mm open-top, 3.5 mm tall, 126 400 in stock).
* **Stack height is calculated from datasheet dimensions** (`tools/verify_stack_height.py`,
  `verification/stack-height.json`): jumper envelope 6.0 mm nominal / 6.6 mm
  worst case (2.0 mm insulator + 4.0 mm mating pin; the 2.8 mm solder tail is
  below the board), module underside 11.0 mm nominal (8.5 mm socket + 2.5 mm
  module male insulator), so the **clearance is 5.0 mm nominal / 3.8 mm worst
  case** against a 0.5 mm target. **Minimum socket insulator height 6.3 mm**
  (swallow the worst-case mating pin), i.e. a **6.6 mm nominal part** once the
  X.X ±0.30 tolerance is applied; the same script proves that no module
  bottom-side footprint overlaps the jumper envelope on any of the 12 slots and
  persists its coverage counts and exceptions.
* `tools/verify_power.py` gains an explicit **routed-PCB SHA pin**
  (`routed_pcb_sha256` in `verification/fixed-geometry.json`) so the release
  cannot be regenerated from a moved board; its report and
  `production/manifest.json` now carry `release_status: fab-ready` plus an
  explicit `physical_validation_pending` list instead of the old hold text.
* `production/cost-estimate.json` (new, generated by `tools/estimate_cost.py`)
  records a sourced **partial** component subtotal: **$21.96/board at a 50-board
  batch** ($19.94 JLCPCB-assembled + $2.01 hand-solder board parts; the 12 shunts
  are a separate $0.12/board accessory order), dominated by 12 x TPS2111APWR at
  ≈$16.45/board. It prices 154 of the 163 designators and lists the nine it
  cannot price **by reference** (5V14, four jacks, two pots, FB1/FB2); PCB
  fabrication and SMT assembly are excluded and need a real quote. Its
  `--selftest` checks the priced/not-priced accounting (7 checks).
* ERC is still the 8-finding baseline (4 `pin_not_connected`, 3
  `power_pin_not_driven`, 1 `lib_symbol_mismatch`); DRC is still 0 errors / 0
  unconnected / 0 schematic-parity with the same 59 retained
  `lib_footprint_mismatch` warnings. The regenerated gerbers/drills differ from
  the committed ones only in their two export-date comment lines each.
* `tools/verify_power.py` now derives `stack_height_verified` from the current
  `verification/stack-height.json` instead of asserting it, and refuses a
  **stale** report: `stack-height.json` carries SHA-256 fingerprints of
  `hand-solder.json`, `fixed-geometry.json`, both schematics, the PCB and all 27
  inspected module PCBs, and `tools/verify_power.py --selftest` proves the guard
  rejects a changed board and a changed module PCB.
* `tools/verify_stack_height.py` now also walks every top-side through-hole
  footprint on all 27 inspected modules (1 332 footprint-per-slot comparisons,
  111 distinct parts) and checks it against the jumper envelopes with the tail
  and module-board tolerances applied (1.4 mm nominal -> 1.86 mm worst-case
  tail, 0.45 mm nearest XY envelope gap, no overlap anywhere). The vertical
  1.94 mm figure is labelled as specific to the assumed 3.0 mm header tail.
* Open items are listed in POWER.md section 9.6: no assembled stack has been
  measured yet (a mating trial is still advised), the module male header part is
  not annotated on the module PCBs, and 4mix/imix place their power headers on
  `F.Cu` where they cannot mate downwards.

## 1.3.1
Structure-only refactor: the twelve identical per-slot power blocks are drawn
once as a hierarchical template sheet (`slot.kicad_sch`) and instantiated twelve
times (`Slot1` … `Slot12`) from `base.kicad_sch`. Nothing electrical changed.

* **No part added, removed, re-valued or re-sourced.** The exported flat netlist
  is unchanged: the same 78 nets with the same nodes, pin functions and pin types
  (proof: `tools/verify_slot_refactor.py` diffing
  `verification/netlist-before.kicadsexpr` against
  `verification/netlist-after.kicadsexpr`; see POWER.md section 8).
* One template instance = one slot: the 1x05 supply socket (`VSUPPLY_n`), the
  1x05 ground socket (`GND_n`), the TPS2111APWR power mux (`U6..U17`), the 750R
  ILIM resistor (`R28, R30 … R50`), the 100k SEL pulldown (`R29, R31 … R51`),
  the 1uF + 0.1uF rail decoupling (`C22 … C45`) and the 1x02 rail-select header
  (`J7..J18`) - exactly what the flat sheet drew per slot.
* Only two nets are slot specific, so the template exposes exactly two sheet
  pins - `VSLOT` and `SEL` - wired on the parent to the existing global labels
  `VSLOT_n` / `SEL_n`. `5V_SYS`, `+3.3V` and `GND` stay global labels / power
  symbols, as before.
* Reference designators are annotated per sheet instance, so every part keeps the
  reference it had in v1.3.0: Slot1 = U6 / J7 / R28 / R29 / C22 / C23 /
  VSUPPLY_1 / GND1, … Slot12 = U17 / J18 / R50 / R51 / C44 / C45 / VSUPPLY_12 /
  GND12.
* ERC is unchanged (same 8 findings: 4 `pin_not_connected`, 3
  `power_pin_not_driven`, 1 `lib_symbol_mismatch`). `production/bom.csv` and the
  JLCPCB BOM/CPL regenerate identically (163 references / 119 SMD components,
  32 BOM rows).
* **`base.kicad_pcb` is intentionally untouched (byte-identical)**; KiCad still
  reports 0 unconnected items and 0 schematic-parity findings against the new
  schematic.
* One file now serves twelve instances, so the free-text `Description` fields
  that named their own slot ("Slot 4 rail decoupling") became instance-neutral.
  No field that reaches the BOM, CPL, netlist or the board changed.
* No LED is added and no electrical decision is reopened.

## 1.3.0
Power upgrade: input protection, a protected 5 V rail and per-slot rail selection.

* **Input protection** at the USB-C connector: **F1** resettable polyfuse
  (BSMD1812-200-16V, 2 A hold / 4 A trip / 16 V) in series with VBUS and TVS **D2**
  (SMAJ5.0A) on the protected node, both upstream of FB1.
* **New protected 5V_SYS rail**, tapped from protected VBUS (after F1, before FB1) with its
  own polyfuse **F2** (MF-MSMF110/16-2, 1.1 A hold / 2.2 A trip / 16 V), TVS **D3**,
  100 uF/16 V + 0.1 uF bulk and an ERC PWR_FLAG. Protection hierarchy: F1 (2 A hold) protects
  the source/wiring, F2 (1.1 A hold) the 5 V branch, U5's own internal limit the 3.3 V branch.
* **Accepted limitation (documented in POWER.md):** the TVS diodes clamp at 9.2 V at peak
  pulse, above the 6 V absolute maximum of the mux inputs and of U5; VBUS_PROT can therefore
  transiently exceed 6 V during a surge. No rated OVP is fitted in v1.3.0; the planned (not
  implemented) mitigation is a rated OVP switch upstream of both branches.
* **12 x per-slot 2:1 power mux** (**U6..U17**, TPS2111APWR, extended part, 2 482 in stock):
  IN1 = 5V_SYS, IN2 = +3.3 V, OUT = the slot rail feeding all five pins of the existing
  `VSUPPLY_n` socket (net `VSLOT_n`). Manual mode (D0 strapped low), reverse and
  cross-conduction blocking. (The input labels are swapped relative to the original brief so
  that the mandated jumper polarity - shunt = 5 V - is preserved; see POWER.md.)
* **Per-slot current limit** `R28, R30, ... R50 = 750 ohm` (ILIM) -> about 667 mA nominal,
  inside the datasheet-guaranteed 0.63-1.25 A adjustment range of the TPS2111A. The
  odd-numbered `R29, R31, ... R51` are the 100 k SEL pulldowns, not part of the limit.
* **Per-slot selection**: `SEL_n` with a 100 k pulldown and a low-profile 1x02 header
  (**J7..J18**, 2.00 mm, hidden under a mated module). **Shunt fitted = 5V_SYS; shunt absent
  or lost = +3.3 V (fail-safe).** Slot current flows through the mux only - the header carries
  the select level, never slot power.
* Per-mux decoupling 1 uF + 0.1 uF on the slot rail.
* **Sourcing**: LCSC/MPN/Manufacturer fields added to every symbol; `production/bom.csv`
  regenerated and every `TO-VERIFY` line closed. FB1/FB2: the designed GZ2012E800TF is not in
  the JLCPCB library, so the fitted part is the 80 ohm-class equivalent **PZ2012D800-3R0TF**
  (C12389: 80 ohm @100 MHz +/-25 %, 3 A, 40 mohm - same impedance class and same 0805 land as
  the design intent, higher current and lower DCR; same-family 1 A alternative C316425
  GZ2012D800TF). Remaining `NOT-JLC` (4 lines, by design, hand-solder): the four 3.5 mm jacks
  and the two RV09 pots; plans recorded in POWER.md. The per-slot 2.00 mm jumper shunts are a
  user-fit accessory, not on the assembly BOM; the matching stocked part is C5664.
* BOM values fixed (the 24 slot sockets were mislabelled `Conn_02x05_Socket` although they are
  1x05; the SPICE sources V1..V3 are no longer offered for assembly).
* No LED is added for 5 V indication (module firmware indicates its own rail).
* U5, the 3.3 V regulator and the **FB1/FB2 circuit** are functionally unchanged (the beads are
  simply ordered as the stocked 80 ohm equivalent noted above). PCB placement/routing is NOT
  part of this revision - see POWER.md for the Phase 2 constraints.

## 1.2.0
Introduce a new LDO (AP2112K-3.3) which has an EN functionality.
So, we can implement the reset button without using additional components.

Due the older design with a transitor, it won't be able to load mutiple MCUs.

